  "question": "For every family whose state depends on a recorded verdict, do the packet bytes that verdict was measured on still exist at HEAD?",
  "whyItMatters": "A FAIL measured on bytes that have since moved is not a repair backlog item. It is an unread packet, and the executable next step is a fresh raster and an independent read rather than a repair shift. Four repair shifts were about to be spent on families in that state.",
  "howItWasMeasured": "For each family, every recorded verifiedAtBase was resolved with git rev-parse <base>:<path> for fixtures/canonical.pdf and fixtures/boundary.pdf and compared to the same paths at HEAD. Blob identity, not a commit count: the generator rewrites product-wiring.json for every admitted family on every run, so counting commits that touched a directory overstates movement badly.",
  "whatThisDoesNotSay": "A moved fixture is not a repaired fixture. This says only that the recorded verdict no longer describes the current bytes. It sets no verdict, promotes nothing and clears no obligation.",
  "counts": {
    "packetBytesMovedSinceTheVerdict": 15,
    "verdictStillDescribesCurrentBytes": 9,
    "notDecidableHere": 9,
    "baseNotReadable": 0
  },
